before:
```typescript
function sizeOf<T extends { length: number }>(value: T): number {
  return value.length;
}
console.log(sizeOf("Seed"));
console.log(sizeOf([4, 8]));
```
This prints 4, then 2. sizeOf accepts a value with numeric length and returns value.length. The constraint after extends makes that property safe to read. "Seed" has four characters; the array has two entries. A custom object supplies its own length; the function does not inspect its contents. console.log displays each returned number.

```typescript
function sizeOf<T extends { length: number }>(value: T): number {
  return value.length;
}
console.log(sizeOf(""));
console.log(sizeOf({ length: 7, label: "Shelf" }));
```
This prints 0, then 7. Trace the changed value through the same rule.

after:
```typescript
function sizeOf<T extends { length: number }>(value: T): number {
  return value.length;
}
console.log(sizeOf("Seed"));
```
This prints `4`. The word `Seed` has four characters. The function reads its `length` and returns that number.
